{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the last-bucket crash. `bucket_of` in `repo/histogram.py` never capped the bucket index. A value sitting exactly on the top edge, like 30 with width 10 and 3 buckets, got index 3 when the last valid index is 2. `histogram_summary` then crashed with an `IndexError`, and that's the error you saw on the last bucket.\n\nThe fix is one line, and it matches what the function's docstring already said it should do:\n\n```python\nreturn min(value // width, n_buckets - 1)\n```\n\nBoth tests pass. I also ran `histogram_summary([0, 5, 10, 29, 30], 10, 3)`, and it now returns counts `[2, 1, 2]`, with the edge value 30 counted in the last bucket.\n\nTwo behaviours to be aware of:\n- **Values above the top:** anything past the range, such as 45, is now also put in the last bucket rather than causing an error.\n- **Negative values:** these still aren't handled. They would give a negative index, which Python quietly treats as counting from the end, so they'd land in the wrong bucket without any error. If your data can go below zero, it's worth deciding whether to clamp them to bucket 0 or reject them.\n\nI didn't touch the unrelated TODOs in `rows.py` and `format.py`."}]},"session_id":"bfd1084e-0da9-4dd3-b30e-beaa7522c5cb"}
